Date: Sunday, 11 July 1993 13:31:04 PST8
From: "James A. Donald" <jamesdon@infoserv.com>
Subject: Technical Nanosystems criticisms

Dear Eric Drexler

I am honored that you have chosen to reply to my criticism
of your work.

My subject line was inappropriate, since it would seem to
imply that your work is entirely worthless and of no
importance, which is of course quite untrue.  I very much
regret the offensive tone of my subject line.

Rather I was seeking to argue that in your eagerness to
advocate rapid progress towards nanotechnology, you ignored
complications that will make workable nanotechnology
harder to design and harder to understand.

If we look at the actual working nanotechnology of living
cells it looks like it is designed for a world dominated by
overwhelming static cohesion, precisely counterbalanced by
overwhelming random brownian motion, the kind of world we
expect from naively and straight forwardly applying scaling
laws.

Scaling laws say that as we go to smaller and smaller
scales, cohesive forces and brownian motion become more
and more important, with cohesion first becoming
overwhelming, then brownian motion overwhelming cohesion. 
Macroscopically we experience cohesive forces as static
friction and static cling, as when one wraps ones lunch
with Glad Wrap.

Your proposed nanotechnology looks like it is designed for
a world where static friction is nonexistent and brownian
motion unimportant, a world very like the macroscopic
world.  It looks like it is expected to work at zero or
very low temperature.  This is not at all what scaling laws
would lead us to expect.

Your basic approach is to consider a component, and show
that for that component, nonuniformities in the potential,
and various imperfections and unpleasantness, are less than
kT.  You then act as if less than kT meant "very small,
effectively zero", and assume that such components can be
treated as perfect, and can be cobbled together as if one
was designing a nanomachine of perfect frictionless
components to work at zero or very low temperature, whereas
in fact there are substantial energy barriers that need to
be overcome by substantial brownian motion.

As you are well aware, on a nanometer scale, kT is not
small.  A molecule whose internal cohesion was merely that
of macroscopic steel would be swiftly and violently
rearranged by such energies.

If one links several nanocomponents to form a larger
nanocomponent, then if the links are flexible the larger
nanocomponent is going to be nondeterministic, and if the
links are nearly rigid, then the combined energy barriers
for the combined system are likely to be substantially
larger than kT with the result that the larger
nanocomponent freezes up.

For a small nanocomponent, one nanometer or less, the
energy barriers will always be less than kT, hence no
static friction for such a component in isolation.

If you rigidly combine several such nanocomponents, the
result is a larger nanocomponent.

For nanocomponents larger than a nanometer, keeping the
energy barriers small means carefully combining the
components in such a way that the energy barriers balance,
rather than adding.  This requires a very high degree of
symmetry, but composite nanocomponents have a lower, not
higher symmetry.

To take a trivial example, if you have two coaxial
bearings, one of which has a periodic potential of period
pi/24 and one of which has a periodic potential of period
pi/25, then there will be some phase at which the energy
barriers are going to add.  If you have a bearing that is
highly symmetric, and it is strained in some direction,
then it loses symmetry, so that effects that are intended
to cancel are likely to add.

You would undoubtedly say that such things are examples of
bad design, and certainly such simple cases are simple to
avoid.  But as nanocomponents are rigidly cobbled together
into larger and larger structures the delicate balancing
act necessary to avoid large energy barriers and consequent
static friction becomes more and more delicate and precise.
Messy complex and high order effects become more and more
important.

A nanomachine composed of rigidly connected nanocomponents
is in effect a single very large rigid molecule, much
larger and less symmetric than the nanocomponents that
compose it.  Thus it is invalid to look at the individual
nanocomponents and say that for each separate
nanocomponent the energy barriers are less than kT,
therefore static friction is zero, therefore no problem.

Drexler said in <9307101011.AA05984@ude.tim.ia.ung.gnu.ai.mit.edu>, drexler@netcom.com:

> >From: "James A. Donald" <jamesdon@infoserv.com>
> >Subject: NANO: Drexler style nanotech is unworkable
> 
> This is a sweeping claim, covering all possible systems of a broad class.


True.  I should not have made such a sweeping claim.  What
I should have said is that nanomachines cannot both have
dry bearings and be rigidly deterministic, hence cannot be
modeled on macro machines, but must instead operate
thermodynamically, like protoplasm.

Number of uncontrolled degrees of freedom times kT must be
greater than the binding energy of the van der Waals forces
that are tending to freeze up the bearings.

Hence no determinism.


> >...Large molecules tend to stay put. 
> 
> Large molecules have large binding energies, but need not have large energy
> barriers to interfacial sliding. This permits good bearings.

> >> I would like to see someone in this group actually present some calculations
> >> rather than just waving their hands. [rjc@gnu.ai.mit.edu (Ray)]
> >
> >No can do.  The maths of van der Waals forces, done
> >properly, is truly awesome...
> 
> "Done properly"? Exactly modeling any many-particle system is impossible,
> but engineers do more than just wave their hands. If one uses standard
> approximations, and can demonstrate insensitivity to their details, then
> the burden of evidence falls on the critic: 

The approximation that you use, that all models for large
molecular structures use, that van der Waals forces are two
particle independently additive, tends to substantially
underestimate the stickiness of van der Waals forces. 
Obviously in a pairwise interaction there is no obstacle to
motion that does not change the radial distance, but in the
real world there is substantial resistance to such motion.

Example:  Attempts to model globular proteins of known
structure. Standard models show them as oil drops, but we
know they are not internally liquid.

Mutant proteins, where one of the interior hydrophobic
amino acids has a smaller side chain usually result in an
empty gap inside the globular protein, not in a slightly
smaller globular protein, contrary to the predictions of
standard modeling approximations.

Such examples show that the biases in assuming that van
der Waals forces are pairwise additive are systematically
in the direction of underestimating the obstacles to
sliding about.  Indeed they cannot be other than that
because all reasonable approximations to van der Waals
forces represent them as purely radial.

It seems to me that in order to argue that scaling
arguments are irrelevant you need to show that friction in
the macro systems being scaled is the result of
imperfections.

A good analog of a nanoscale proposed bearing is a macro
bearing lubricated by hot HOPG in a vacuum.  I have heard
that HOPG is only effective as a lubricant in the presence
of air.  Presumably absorbed air molecules, or absorbed
contaminants, become entrained between layers.  Obviously
HOPG is not perfect, but if friction was the result of
imperfections we would expect the near perfection of better
grades of HOPG to result in a radical decrease in the shear
strength of the HOPG, which is obviously not the case. 
HOPG is HOPG as far a shear strength goes.

> >Consider one carbon nanotube inside another...

(Drexler points out that typical nanotubes are unlikely to be perfect 
at the ends.)

> > Therefore, by Drexler's method of
> > calculations, their surrounding "force fields" will have
