I do not confuse them.  I point out that high order
effects couple them - that due to non linearity the beat
pattern between two high spatial frequency nonuniformities
will produce a low spatial frequency nonuniformity, which
is harder to shield out or design away.

> -- *unless* the point is that this pattern will
> interact with immediately adjacent asymmetrical features in the environment
> (the "surface supporting the tube"). Such intrusive asymmetries, however,
> are not part of well designed nanomechanical bearings. 


A well designed nanomechanical bearing will indeed be
perfectly symmetric if it simply floats in space like those
beautiful diagrams of nanomechanical bearings constructed
by molecular modeling.  But a bearing has to be part of a
large messy complicated highly asymmetric object, which
will induce asymmetry in the bearing.  As part of a large,
effectively macroscopic object, it will suffer the
complicated and obscure imperfections that macroscopic
objects suffer from.

In my nanotube example the outer tube does not shield the
inner tube from asymmetry in its environment - on the
contrary the beat pattern between the outer tube and the
graphite surface on which the outer tube is resting
*creates* a large scale (low spatial frequency) non
uniformity from a small scale (high spatial frequency) non
uniformity.

The point is that asymmetries cannot easily be shielded
out, that everything tends to couple to everything else,
that high spatial frequency forces couple to low spatial
frequency forces (harder to shield) through nonlinear
effects, that the micro world is messier than the
macroworld, that perfect bearings are therefore harder, not
easier, to build, the ultimate imperfection being the
finite size and spacing of individual atoms, that in
consequence attempting to turn such a bearing will produce
a lot of bumping and shaking.

None of this *proves* that dry bearings will not work. The
vague complicated and messy effects that I describe cannot
be "proven" mathematically to be significant.  Rather, I am
pointing out that we have no strong reason to doubt scaling
law arguments.

The smallest human built motors look nothing like
macroscopic motors. The "motors" employed by cells look
even less like macroscopic motors, being designed for a
world where thermal forces are of the same order as
cohesive forces, and systematic and large scale structural
forces are vastly smaller than cohesive (static friction
and static cling) forces, a world where inertia is
insignificant.  The designs that cells use look like they
are designed for a world in which the scaling law arguments
do indeed hold.

> > Drexler used the model that the forces were pairwise
> > additive independent. Just about everyone uses this model
> > because it is the only way one can do calculations, not
> > because it gives realistic results.
> 
> There are several companies selling fiendishly expensive molecular modeling
> software to real chemists trying to understand real molecules in the
> laboratory. This software uses the pairwise additivity approximation, and
> gives results generally regarded as realistic because they match experiment
> quite well.

They match experiment quite well in circumstances where
slipperiness is not important.  They model the interior of
protein molecules quite badly.

People only use this modeling software for the first cut
in rational drug design.  Then they check the first cut by
doing an X ray diffraction on the complex formed by the
molecule and its receptor to see how the drug actually fits
with the molecule it is designed to fit into.  In the
recent rational design of an influenza drug they found,
and expected to find, substantial and unwanted gaps and
mismatches.

The existence of gaps, despite the large cohesive forces
tending to squash everything together, shows that modeling
software substantially underestimates resistance to non
radial motion.  (The nanoscale equivalent of static
friction.)

> To be a bit more quantitative, J. Israelachvilli (Intermolecular and
> Surface Forces, Academic Press, 1992, p.106) states that the effect of
> nonpairwise additivity on the energy (relative to the results of an
> uncorrected pairwise calculation) "can be positive or negative and is
> usually small, less than 20%,

If the binding energy varies 20% as the bearing rotates, 
then that bearing will not rotate.

> Very well. Your "description" is purely verbal, although it refers to
> mathematical concepts. Could you please present a concrete mathematical
> model of a bearing of the sort that Nanosystems predicts to work, but
> refuting that conclusion by showing rotational energy barriers large
> compared to kT? 

Sure - just as soon as I have finished a small program
that can compare the energies of qualitatively different
protein folds with an accuracy better than kT (just joking)

> I will accept any plausible simplification that makes the
> calculation easier. Coplanar ring geometries (Nanosystems, pp.285-289) with
> less than 20 atoms should suffice to show your proposed effect and will
> result in small computational costs for almost any three-body potential.


20 Atoms?

Interesting choice of scale.  You and I both know that for
structures that small, scaling laws predict that static
friction will be much less than the forces arising from
brownian motion, or to use language more appropriate to
that scale, the energy barriers to rotation will be much
smaller than kT for reasonable bearings.

A bearing that small will indeed not freeze up.  It will
have no problem rotating freely.  Quite the reverse.
Suppose that bearing is holding a shaft that transmits
torque from one part of the machine to another. A long
shaft so thin will be twisted up like telephone cord by
thermal disturbances, and you have motion, but not
determinism.

Also a long axle that thin will be dragged against the
walls of its housing by van der Waals forces, so you will
have both random motion, because a long shaft that thin
will twist, and complicated sticking, because it will have
an off center, hence asymmetric, bonding with the shaft
housing, and the shaft housing, being part of an object
that does not have radial symmetry about the shaft, will
itself not have cylindrical symmetry and will impose a
highly asymmetric force, involving hundreds or thousands of
atoms in contact between the shaft and the shaft housing. 
One does not need accurate molecular modeling to predict
that such a contact will lock up part of the shaft solidly.

Thus while the small bearing you describe will spin
beautifully with no problems, and one does not need
precise molecular modelling to see that, the much larger
and less symmetric nanomachine of which they are part will
lock up, while the bearings continue to spin beautifully.

One solution is to make the entire axle shaft one large
bearing.  Thus instead of dealing with a twenty atom
bearing, ten atoms in circumference and two atoms thick,
we are dealing with a thousand atom bearing, ten atoms in
circumference and one hundred atoms long.

I think you will agree that such a large bearing is likely
to lock up solidly.  The total van der Waals potential is
so very large that any small angular variation, inevitable
when the circumference is only ten atoms and the rest of
the nearby machine is not cylindrically symmetric, is going
to be vastly larger than kT.

Now if the bearing housing was totally isolated from
everything, we could stagger it uniformly and ensure that
everything canceled out perfectly.  But near the ends it
cannot be isolated from everything, furthermore it is going
to flex significantly, producing another horde of symmetry
breaking complications, complications that would be
obviously insignificant in a twenty atom bearing, but
seem likely to be fifty times larger in a thousand atom
bearing.

Of course we could easily beat this problem by giving it a
larger circumference to match its long length, make it one
hundred atoms in circumference instead of ten.  This solves
the grab against the walls problem, and bumps in the
potential problem caused by high order effects that I have
been talking about, but then the arguments of Hal Finney,
hfinney@shell.portal.com become applicable.  An axle that
large will suffer local flexing of an Angstrom or so under
van der Waals forces, destroying the cancellation, and so
it will stick Glad Wrap fashion, in the manner that Hal
Finney describes.

In short:

The particular components of nanomachines are molecular
scale and highly symmetric, so you can rightly ignore the
nasty complications that we lump together macroscopically
as static friction.

The behavior of any one component will be perfect, because
its imperfections are less than kT.

The atom scale illustrations in books on nanotechnology
show bearings floating in splendid isolation.  If they
floated in splendid isolation you would not need bearings.

When you rigidly lock a large number of such components
together you get a small macroscopic object, highly
asymmetric.  Some of the imperfections will randomly cancel
out.  Some will systematically cancel out.  Some will
randomly add due asymmetry, and some will systematically
add, due to large scale orderly asymmetry.

As a result the small macroscopic object will suffer the
ills that afflict macroscopic objects of that size - in
particular it will suffer from ills that we macroscopically
average out and call static friction.

The solution to the problem of static friction is
lubricant, (on that scale lubricant is solvent) or internal
flexibility to decouple the components.  Either solution
will tend to reduce or eliminate determinism. Both
solutions together equal something very like protoplasm.

I fully agree that nanomachines are possible, and that they
can be built in ways that make them a great deal easier to
understand than protoplasm.  But if they are build like
rigid clockwork macroscopic machinery then they will act
like rigid clockwork macroscopic machinery, and normal
scaling laws will apply.

