> to do the shipping, because of the benefits you mention 
> of nonlocal product and component manufacture.

The relative decrease in transport & trading costs have been on the
slide for at a least a century and probably longer.  I don't see why
or how the introduction general assembly mechanisms will alter this.

> Today, when I purchase a hot tub it is not delivered with water
> already inside.  That's because there's lots of locally-available
> water

Hmm.  My last project was in the water distribution business.  In
England and Wales (and probably Scotland) aqueducts span many miles and
water is not locally "produced".  For example water is transported
across the Welsh/English border.  Reservoirs in Wales supply parts of
England.  Of course there's nothing to stop individuals from collecting
rainwater (and some people do) but I suspect they do this for largely
personal, non-monetary reasons (just as many people spend many
relatively unproductive (but happy) hours growing their own vegetables
(some) when they could purchase them more cheaply from supermarkets).

You say you locally complete the addition of hot water.  Do you also
have a solar cells to heat the rainwater?  And if you do is it cost-
justified?

>> Product complexity in manufacturing is added a stage at a 
>> time, by assembly processes that are _more_ complex than the 
>> complexity (value) they're adding.  The finished product may
>> still be more complex than any individual piece of machinery 
>> in the assembly process.
> 
> Well, the idea is that a self-replicating GA changes the game.

It doesn't change the point I'm making above; complexity is best added
in small incremental steps by an array of different assembler complexes
/ processes, each specialising in a particular stage of the production
sequence.  Each stage in the split assembly process achieves much
greater throughput than a general assembler because the form of the
assembler is suited to match a particular manufacturing sub-function.

> still not quite sure what it is you are arguing -- whether the GA
> approach won't be the dominant manufacturing technology because
> it's too difficult to achieve "general" assemblers, or because they'd
> be too inefficient to compete with specialized production facilities.

Both.

Here's what I believe:

1)   A GA is possible, but not as hordes of self-replicating GAs in a
     vat.  The co-ordination problems make this approach, at the very
     least, highly difficult, certainly uneconomic and possibly
     impossible.  Rather a GA would be a single, integrated entity which
     would undergo internal transformation specific to the product
     requirement at hand, fed in as a high level command and interpreted
     by an embedded AI function.  What we might call the genie-in-the-
     lamp approach.
2)   Simple or personalised products may be locally produced by a GA.
3)   Complex products will be cheaper to purchase from the market, where
     they are assembled stage-by-stage, at different global locations,
     using conventional mass-production methodologies.
4)   Complex personalised products will always be beyond the local GA
     production capability.  Note: I would hope that the distinction
     between simple and complex is a moving target so that steadily more
     complex products become capable of local production (ie we upgrade
     our GAs).  I expect that consumer demand being what it is, local
     demand for complexity will always oustrip local capabilities - ie
     that there will always be a demand for global (and beyond) produce.

I simply do not see (after some considerable thought) how self-
replication helps the manufacturing process, either locally or globally. 
The problems involved in centrally co-ordinating, soviet-style,
trillions of self-replicating GAs and preventing them from evolving into
some self-directed grey goo, in addition to the lower efficiency that
general assembly implies, make this approach unworkable.

In addition I believe that the Frankenstein grey-goo nightmare of self-
replicating GAs has a negative impact on the acceptance of
nanotechnology, with the resultant cries to suppress nanotech to "save
the world" or bring it all under the heavy hand of the state, in the
"national interest".

Viewing nanotechnology as simply an extension of existing
microtechnologies removes many of these fears of economic upheaval or
sui-genocide.  It also means that the path to nanotech does not require
any special (read: state-funded) research drive.  Just let the market
drive the current technologies (sub-micron chip fabrication, genetic and
bio-engineering, optico-electronics, quantum dots, STMs, SETs etc) and
nanotechnology will arrive, not with a bang but with a whimper..... as
sure as eggs is eggs.

