Date:     Tue, 13 Jul 93 11:05:47 GMT
From: starr@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Subject: Extropian Symbols

Methinks a big part of the noise about our suspended topic has to do with
symbolism more than anything else.  Extropianism has tended towards highly
abstract symbols thus far: geometrical shapes, artificial colors, etc.

In a way, this lends itself to the nature v. artifice false dichotomy, by
the emphasis upon artifice to the exclusion of the "natural."  (I place
the term "natural" in quotes in the last sentence because, since artifice
is part of the nature of the kind of animals we are, just like dam-building
is part of the nature of beavers, the artificial IS truly natural; it's a
subset of the class of natural things.)

We may gain by adopting more "naturalistic" symbols.  Those of us that have
read Victor Koman's delightful book "The Jehovah Contract" may recall how
the author portrays patriarchal religion in conflict with matriarchal
religion as an ideology of war and death v. one of peace and life.  Surely
we could use a symbol of the longevity included in extropianism!  We don't
have to reinvent all wheels; the spontaneous order and tradition of human
poetry, ritual, and ceremony has provided us with things that may serve
the purpose, such as the mythical Phoenix.  Imagine an Extropy symbol with
a Pheonix in the middle, with the spinning arrows also indicating the flames
which bring about its rebirth!

For those who insist upon hostility to attempts to explore this part of our
common human heritage in the name of science, answer me this question: what
was the religion of the ancient Greeks and Romans, who originated science?

Much of this also seems to stem from a false dichotomy between two points of
view: first-person, and third-person.  Most science is done from the latter,
but much poetry, ritual and ceremony from the former.  These are different
points of view, and yield different "results," some incompatible with each
other, but not all.

I repeat: not all observations from one of these two points of view are
incompatible with those observations that can be made from the other point of
view.

It continues to amaze me that so many of us tend to get "stuck" in the third-
person point of view, when we should have learned better from our study of
spontaneous order and the critiques of this point of view that come from
this school of thought such as Hayek.  He called it "scientism."

Furthermore, even the incompatibility of observations from different points of
view doesn't necessarily entail that we should abandon one or the other.  Nor
does this necessarily entail that if we accept this that we are committed to
the position that truth is relative, either.  If you've already read W.W.
Bartley III's works on epistemology, and you think otherwise, then perhaps
your opinion would become more informed by reading David Kelley's, especially
"The Evidence of the Senses: A Realist Theory of Perception."

